  "summary": "Africa's earliest sculpture tradition: serene life-sized terracotta heads with triangular eyes.",
  "description": "Nok terracottas — naturalistic heads and figures with piercing triangular eyes, elaborately coiffed hair, beads and basketry hats — appear from around 1500 BCE to 500 CE on the Jos plateau, West Africa's earliest known sculptural tradition and contemporaneous with early iron smelting. Farmers uncovered them in tin-mining gravel; archaeologists (Bernard Fagg above all) pieced the culture together through thermoluminescence dating. Fragmentary, weathered, and haunting, Nok heads anchor Nigerian museums and every survey of African art history.",
  "culturalContext": "Breunig and Rupp (2016) stated: \"Their origin is unknown, but since the plants they used as crops (especially millet) are indigenous to the Sahel region, a northern homeland is more probable than any other.\" Breunig (2017) expounded: \"The people of the Nok culture must have come from somewhere else. So far, however, we have not found out what region, though we suspect the Sahel zone in West Africa.\" Champion et al. (2022) suggested that they may have come from the Central Sahara, and stated:\n\nThe cultivation of pearl millet diffused from the desiccating West and Central Sahara into the West African savanna zone after 2500 BCE, in the context of southwards population movements (Ozainne et al. 2014; Neumann 2018; Fuller et al. 2021)…The presence of pearl millet without roulette decorations or chaff temper, as seen in the Nok and early Gajiganna sites, suggests that the third diffusion originated directly from the central Sahara and possibly split before reaching northern Nigeria, accounting for the differences in Nok and Gajiganna pottery (Fig. 8; and see Champion 2020, p. 462).",
